title: syslog.tail_lines(0) returns the whole file, so hark logs -n 0 and watch-logs -n 0 dump everything
updated_at: "2026-10-26T00:13:15Z"
---
Found by the B186 implementation agent, 2026-08-23, and independently reproduced.

src/hark/syslog.py tail_lines() does `lines = data.splitlines()[-n:]`. For n=0 that is `[-0:]` == `[0:]` == the ENTIRE file, because -0 == 0 in Python. So:

- `hark watch-logs -n 0` dumps all of system.jsonl, while its own --help says "0=none"
- `hark logs -n 0` does the same

Reproduced directly: a 5-record jsonl gives tail_lines(0) -> 5 records, tail_lines(2) -> 2 records.

Fix: guard `if n <= 0: return []` at the top of tail_lines, matching hark.transcripts.tail_records which already does exactly that (added in B186). Then check the callers to make sure "0" reads as "none" rather than as "default" anywhere.

Also worth a look while in there: the same `[-n:]` shape appears in the read-block loop condition (`while size > 0 and data.count(b"\n") <= n`), which for n=0 reads only one block. Not a correctness bug once n<=0 returns early, but do not leave a second path that behaves differently.

Not fixed in B186 - correctly held as out of scope by the implementer.

Implementation/decision receipt 2026-10-26: tail_lines returns empty for n<=0 before filesystem access; logs zero no longer reads whole file. watch-logs already guarded. Independent review and parent review completed; isolated-worktree code merged to master. Final combined remote gate pending.